# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/

# Environments
#
# venv/ is the in-repo CPython 3.6.7 execution environment for AirSense V1.
# It is a conda-style prefix built by micromamba, roughly 530 MB, and it is
# deliberately NOT tracked: it is a build artifact, not source. What makes it
# reproducible is requirements-v1-2019.txt (direct pins) plus
# requirements-v1-2019-lock.txt (pre-cutoff transitive closure), and what
# makes it verifiable is artifacts/runtime_snapshot.json. Rebuild it with the
# recipe in docs/PHASE_00A_RUNTIME_RECORD.md section 14.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# .venv-v2/ is the AirSense V2 modern execution environment (CPython 3.11).
# Like V1's venv/ it is a build artifact, not source: what makes it
# reproducible is requirements-v2-core.txt plus requirements-v2-core-lock.txt.
.venv-v2/

# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# OS
.DS_Store

# NOTE: data/raw/ is intentionally NOT ignored.
# The raw dataset is tracked so that its SHA-256, recorded in
# artifacts/data_audit.json, can be verified against the file that the
# repository actually carries. Raw files are read-only by convention and
# are never modified in place.

# data/processed/ is the Phase-2 canonical data layer. It is NOT tracked,
# for one practical reason and one principled one.
#
# Practical: data/processed/phase2/sample_index/train.csv is 107.6 MB, above
# GitHub's 100 MB per-file hard limit, so the tree cannot be pushed with it.
#
# Principled: this layer is *derived*, not source. It is regenerated
# deterministically from the tracked data/raw/ by
# scripts/build_forecast_dataset.py, and the SHA-256 of every file it
# produces is recorded in artifacts/v2_phase2_manifest.json. A clone can
# therefore rebuild the layer and *prove* it is byte-identical to the one the
# study used, which is a stronger guarantee than carrying the bytes would be.
#
# Rebuild before running the validators:
#   .venv-v2/bin/python scripts/build_forecast_dataset.py
data/processed/
